1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,400
This is how the raw material
for our doormats is gathered.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,300
The coconut the fibre comes from
comes from here.

3
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:09,400
Those doormats say Made in India.

4
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:10,500
They don't say who made them.

5
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:13,300
Or that for each one,
the women get eighty cents.

6
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:14,500
Four euros a day.

7
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:17,300
From today, every time you see one,
you'll know where it comes from.

8
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:26,300
I'm in the south of India,
in Anathalavattom, Kerala.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,800
Coir is the fibre of the coconut husk.

10
00:00:30,410 --> 00:00:32,880
In Kerala, for generations,
it was worked by hand.

11
00:00:33,380 --> 00:00:37,060
Mostly by women: even today,
in India, about eight in ten.

12
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:41,500
These women used to work
coconut fibre, coir.

13
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,300
Now it's all done by machines,
and they don't do it anymore.

14
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,000
They're pointing us to where it's done now.

15
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,000
Since 1975.

16
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:53,200
The longer I stay, the more women come
who used to work here.

17
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:56,700
They started in the seventies.

18
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,100
Today none of them does it.

19
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,300
They point us to the only place around here
where it's still done.

20
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,300
Maybe we've found a place where...

21
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:06,500
it isn't individuals who do it,
but a society.

22
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,000
But the beautiful thing
is that there's no owner,

23
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:13,200
just a society made of people.
A kind of community

24
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:17,100
that comes together to keep
this ancient work going.

25
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:22,300
Once, along this coast, there were many more
coir societies. Many have closed.

26
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,800
This one has held on for forty years.

27
00:01:25,860 --> 00:01:30,100
He takes the boat to collect the husks,
which are all under water.

28
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:38,390
He brings them here, to the women who open them,
they take out the fibre, the coir,

29
00:01:39,010 --> 00:01:40,410
and make all these bundles.

30
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,640
Now we go to collect the husks
that will be used later.

31
00:01:48,730 --> 00:01:51,700
It all starts here, in the water.

32
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:55,470
The husks stay under the lagoon for six months
to a year, until the fibre comes loose.

33
00:01:56,110 --> 00:01:58,650
We have to be careful
not to hit this other boat.

34
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:02,600
He is seventy-six.

35
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,620
Every day he goes into the water
and pulls up the husks, by hand.

36
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:11,620
This boat is older than the society.
Fifty years old.

37
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:17,220
He goes in up to his chest, feels for the husks
on the bottom and loads them, one after another.

38
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:24,000
And down here, exactly a week ago,
they put the last husks in to soak,

39
00:02:24,100 --> 00:02:28,500
for the six months they told us about,
right down here, in this lagoon.

40
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:32,600
And it's not just coconuts: there's also this fruit,
called poison fruit.

41
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:39,900
He tells us that if you eat it, it's like being poisoned,
or you fall into a deep sleep.

42
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,700
These bundles are husks just pulled out
of the water. Inside is the fibre, ready to be opened.

43
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:50,140
I feel truly privileged to see this work
with my own eyes, such an old tradition.

44
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:53,600
They're teasing me:
"Can I go to Italy with this boat?"

45
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:58,700
They open the husks one by one, sitting on the ground,
and pull out the fibre.

46
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:00,700
Under this sun, work goes on until two.

47
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:05,210
One sharp blow, and the husk opens. Then the fibre is pulled
out by hand. And again, with the next husk.

48
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,100
How many years have you done this?

49
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:10,100
Forty years.

50
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,500
What's your name? "Premilla."

51
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:15,500
How old are you? "Sixty."

52
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:18,500
And how many years have you done this job?
"Forty."

53
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,700
The society opened forty years ago.

54
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:23,900
When this society opened,
she was already here.

55
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,700
And they were the first,
the only women working coconut here.

56
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:33,500
For forty years they've worked here,
making this gesture every day.

57
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:39,270
I hope the camera shows the effort,
the amount of coconut there is.

58
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:43,400
It's hard to communicate, they don't speak
English of course: I need the interpreter.

59
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:46,400
How much is the pay?
"Four hundred rupees a day."

60
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,700
With pay this low,
young people don't come.

61
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:56,820
The wage is four hundred rupees a day:
basically, four euros.

62
00:03:57,330 --> 00:04:00,610
And that's why the new generation
doesn't want to come.

63
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:07,130
And he told me he worries
that this factory, if we can call it that,

64
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:13,360
will stop going on the moment
there are no new people to do it.

65
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:20,750
And so this whole culture, this tradition
of coir, of coconut fibre, will be broken.

66
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:24,210
Because no one will want to do it
for wages this low.

67
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:33,700
Out in the sun work ends at two, but in the factory,
which we're going to see now, at four.

68
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:41,600
From here they go into the machines,
and then they're ready to strip the coconut fibre.

69
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:46,700
From the lagoon, the fibre goes to the machines.
Then into the factory, where it becomes rope.

70
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:51,100
The machines beat and clean the fibre.
Here too, it's women who load them by hand.

71
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:57,600
Two different strands, then they're joined, knotted,
and that makes one rope.

72
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,700
280 ropes a day, in 8 hours of work.

73
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:03,330
Two strands, twisted while walking backwards.

74
00:05:03,830 --> 00:05:06,500
One piece, then another.
Two hundred and eighty in a day.

75
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:12,000
One holds the strands, the other walks backwards. The rope
grows one step at a time, to the end of the shed.

76
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:17,280
This is Mary. In the factory, work goes on until four.
And tomorrow morning it starts again.

77
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:21,500
To then work it by hand, making ropes.

78
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:26,600
Then they make doormats
that are shipped to Europe.

79
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,390
So basically they make
doormats out of coconut fibre.

80
00:05:30,050 --> 00:05:35,900
From these ropes come the doormats that end up
in our homes. In Italy, in France, in Germany.

81
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:40,550
They say Made in India.

82
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:42,730
Now you know who made them.

83
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:55,700
I want to stop on the price of the doormat:
we all know what one costs in Europe.

84
00:05:55,900 --> 00:06:03,480
Here instead, for one doormat, they get
only eighty rupees. Eighty cents.

85
00:06:13,730 --> 00:06:19,630
A centuries-old tradition that's being lost.
I came here, to India, to document it.

86
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:24,300
India produces more than two thirds of the world's coir.
But here, handwork is coming to an end.

87
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,400
Not because no one knows how: because young people
no longer come here, for four euros a day.

88
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,480
These women have carried it on for forty years.

89
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,800
Today, for a few minutes, the voice was theirs.

90
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:41,900
The Voci Vere journey, giving a voice to those
who need it most, goes on. Follow me for the next stories.

